  "difficulty_rating": 1700,
  "explanation": "`bool(value)` on a string only cares whether the string is empty — any non-empty string, including the literal text \"false\" or \"0\", is truthy in Python. So parse_flag(\"false\") is True and parse_flag(\"0\") is True; only the empty string \"\" comes back False. Of the four rows (\"false\", \"\", \"true\", \"0\"), three are counted as enabled instead of the one the data actually intends, because the function never compares the string's *content* against \"true\", only whether it's non-empty.",
  "prompt": "parse_flag is meant to turn a stored string flag into a real boolean. Step through count_enabled and predict what it returns for these four rows.",
  "language": "python",
  "snippet": "def parse_flag(value):\n    return bool(value)\n\ndef count_enabled(rows):\n    count = 0\n    for row in rows:\n        flag = parse_flag(row.get(\"enabled\"))\n        if flag:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\nrows = [{\"enabled\": \"false\"}, {\"enabled\": \"\"}, {\"enabled\": \"true\"}, {\"enabled\": \"0\"}]\nprint(count_enabled(rows))",
